Subject: [PATCH 0003] cuphydriver: hand HARQ pages back to the driver while the cell runs

Patch 0002 put the virtual addresses of large allocations in our hands. This uses
them: it releases the physical pages behind free HARQ buffers and takes them
again, with the cell carrying full traffic throughout.

The interlock already existed and nobody had used it. A buffer sitting in the
free ring has been returned by its callback, so nothing references it and its
ref_count is zero. Dequeueing it and releasing its pages is therefore safe with
no new drain protocol at all -- the ring IS the drain, for this case. What needed
adding was only the ability to drop pages without destroying the address, which
0002 provided.

  HarqPool::retireFree(n)      dequeue up to n free buffers, release their pages
  HarqPool::restoreRetired(n)  map pages again, return the buffers to the ring
  HarqPool::retiredCount()     buffers whose address is alive with no memory behind it

vmm_alloc gains release_physical() and restore_physical(). The virtual address
stays reserved across both, so nothing held by a CUDA graph, a descriptor table
or a device kernel is disturbed; only the memory moves. Region now also captures
the CUmemAllocationProp at allocation time, because a restore may be driven from
a thread that has no CUDA context of its own and must not re-query the device.

Measured, one 4T4R 100 MHz cell at full F08 load, retiring and restoring while
traffic flowed:

    step              GPU        mapped     DL / UL Mbps        CRC
    baseline          4,971 MiB  756 MiB    1,369.97 / 498.22   0
    retire 40         4,811      596        1,369.97 / 498.22   0
    retire 80 more    4,279       64        1,369.97 / 498.22   0
    restore all       4,971      756        1,369.97 / 498.22   0

692 MiB returned to the driver and taken back, throughput never moving, zero CRC
errors, late slots 1 to 2 across the whole run which is baseline noise. The
allocator's own mapped-bytes counter tracks the nvidia-smi figure exactly.

The control path here is a file poller, deliberately crude: it is a test harness,
not the API. The real one belongs on the OAM gRPC service, executed by the
oam_cell_update thread, off the real-time cores. The watcher retains a primary
context because it drives VMM calls, and catches exceptions so that a failed
resize can never take the L1 down -- an earlier revision threw out of the thread
and terminated the process.

Limits worth stating. Only free buffers can be retired, so this shrinks the pool
without touching anything in use, and the pool will starve if asked to give back
more than the traffic leaves idle. Each buffer is still its own reservation, so
granularity is paid per buffer rather than per arena; packing many buffers into
pages that retire as a unit is a later patch. Nothing here helps buffers that are
in flight, which is where a real drain protocol will be needed.

